    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Understanding Address"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Pointer is a powerful feature of C programming that allows us to work with memory addresses."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Before we learn about pointers, let's first learn about addresses."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Suppose we have created a variable like this:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "c",
      "content": "int var;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Now, a space will be allocated in memory for the var variable and we can access the memory address using &var."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Let's see an example."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "c",
      "content": "1#include <stdio.h>\n2\n3int main() {\n4\n5  int var = 13;\n6\n7  printf(\"Value of var: %d\\n\", var);\n8  printf(\"Address of var: %p\", &var);\n9\n10  return 0;\n11}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Output"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "content": "Value of var: 13\nAddress of var: 0x7fffbf58c034"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here, you can see &var results 0x7fffbf58c034 which is the memory location where 13 is stored."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "And, we have used the %p format specifier to print the memory address."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "If you remember, we have used &var with the scanf() function as well. This is because we want to store the input value at the address of var."
    },
